**Problem.** Producers add items to a bounded buffer and consumers remove them. A producer must wait when the buffer is full, and a consumer must wait when it is empty. There must be no lost updates or corrupted state.

**With one lock.** Use one mutex and two condition variables.

```cpp
std::mutex m;
std::condition_variable notFull, notEmpty;
std::queue<int> q;
const size_t CAP = 1024;

void produce(int x) {
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lk(m);
    notFull.wait(lk, [&]{ return q.size() < CAP; });
    q.push(x);
    notEmpty.notify_one();
}
int consume() {
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lk(m);
    notEmpty.wait(lk, [&]{ return !q.empty(); });
    int x = q.front(); q.pop();
    notFull.notify_one();
    return x;
}
```

The predicate form (`while` loop) protects against **spurious wakeups** and lost signals. Waiting releases the mutex atomically. Do not hold the lock while doing work on the item.

**With no locks (single producer, single consumer).** A ring buffer where only the producer writes `tail` and only the consumer writes `head`.

```cpp
bool push(const T& v) {
    size_t t = tail.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
    if (t - head.load(std::memory_order_acquire) == N) return false;  // full
    buf[t % N] = v;
    tail.store(t + 1, std::memory_order_release);   // publish the item
    return true;
}
bool pop(T& out) {
    size_t h = head.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
    if (h == tail.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) return false;      // empty
    out = buf[h % N];
    head.store(h + 1, std::memory_order_release);   // free the slot
    return true;
}
```

The release store makes the item write visible before the index update, and the acquire load pairs with it. Put `head` and `tail` on separate cache lines to avoid **false sharing**, and use a power-of-two size so `% N` becomes a mask.

**Caveats.** This only works for one producer and one consumer. For multiple producers or consumers you need CAS (compare-and-swap) based designs, such as a bounded MPMC queue with per-slot sequence numbers. "Lock-free" queues usually spin or return false, so choose backoff, or park threads, based on latency needs.
